steering committtee of the CDU have announced Bavarian politician Ilse Aigner as presidential candidate. This gives her a good chance of becoming the first woman to occupy the role.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Germany: Chancellor Merz to nominate Aigner for president</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d8f98e02b52f22c4a86f94070c77e62bcf1ab314b84d8ed4ceaa1bfcd055ac74</id><updated>2026-11-09T18:56:18Z</updated><published>2026-11-08T19:43:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-chancellor-merz-to-nominate-aigner-for-president/live-79883533--d347f58eed72.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-chancellor-merz-to-nominate-aigner-for-president/live-79883533?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">German Chancellor Friedrich Merz announced that he will nominate Ilse Aigner for federal president, paving the way for her to be the first woman to hold the office. DW has more.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>37 years after fall of Berlin Wall: How divided are Germans?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d87251254fd35807e1f631b6551e81f017de6826e020f8cf6d794f8179b74860</id><updated>2026-11-09T18:57:40Z</updated><published>2026-11-09T13:59:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/37-years-after-fall-of-berlin-wall-how-divided-are-germans/a-79877461--471b79fce1fe.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/37-years-after-fall-of-berlin-wall-how-divided-are-germans/a-79877461?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Three artists share their thoughts on the reunification of East and West Germany. How do they perceive a shared "we" and what gives them cause for optimism?</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry><entry><title>Who is Ilse Aigner, leading candidate for Germany's next president?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ba31fbe6a031958fb152f10f6faf6e892a6cd42773ef814982c9700174b143b0</id><updated>2026-11-09T18:57:52Z</updated><published>2026-11-09T11:11:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/who-is-ilse-aigner-leading-candidate-for-germany-s-next-president/a-79898848--5540ac90be2d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/who-is-ilse-aigner-leading-candidate-for-germany-s-next-president/a-79898848?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">She would be the first female president and the first Christian Social Union politician to hold Germany's highest office. Ilse Aigner is the favored candidate for the Federal Convention vote on January 30.</summary><category term="Germany" /></entry><entry><title>'Never Again!': Holocaust remembrance under pressure</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e1f89635f9f83b5d7cdb19f9812f06acb1656d7f3f77061387027faf067769f4</id><updated>2026-11-09T18:56:18Z</updated><published>2026-11-08T09:54:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/never-again-holocaust-remembrance-under-pressure/a-79840894--861f4756e8c3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/never-again-holocaust-remembrance-under-pressure/a-79840894?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Only a few Holocaust survivors remain who can share their stories firsthand. Meanwhile, voices calling for an end to what they describe as a "cult of guilt" are growing louder. How can Holocaust remembrance survive?</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry><entry><title>The dachshund, a humorous icon with political symbolism</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fcad094f4a9f7f57f486e175adf4a7fddc27e16b2628019f32c6322923422652</id><updated>2026-11-09T18:56:18Z</updated><published>2026-10-30T07:57:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/the-dachshund-a-humorous-icon-with-political-symbolism/a-79640191--0ec0144d606e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/the-dachshund-a-humorous-icon-with-political-symbolism/a-79640191?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">From Picasso to Andy Warhol, a surprising number of artists have portrayed the German breed. But the dachshund has also at times been co-opted as a political symbol.</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry></feed>
